    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Well, Pete, the district that was drawn by the General Assembly that I would have run in would have been fine. The one that has come out now in this aftermath is fine for me. But the process that we're engaged in and the role of the North Carolina Supreme Court, as you are well aware of my view of that is simply shocking. And I've read the 250 page opinion, well, collection of opinions that came out of the majority and dissent And, you know, there's no language in the state constitution that they draw on to throw out the General Assembly's maps. The President from the Supreme Court says that, of course, our Constitution assigns districting pass to a political body, and so political considerations can be taken into account. They threw all of that out. And without any basis in any law, they just declared that There can be no, essentially, no partisanship. It's got to be a map that is effectively a proportional representation. You got to have the districts drawn so that political parties can win the same proportion of the seats as they purport to have the vote in recent elections. And it's an outrageous power grab And I think it's something that's got to be fixed. I mean, I think the Supreme Court of North Carolina, which is thankfully elected, needs to be changed electorally. And then I think we probably need to go beyond that and make sure that, you know, once the leftists who sue until blue found the federal courts were becoming somewhat resistant to some of their techniques that now switch to close partisan controlled state, Supreme courts. And I think it's a danger to the to democracy people are not that they're just absolutely overruling democratically elected by",
